WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:12.160
Between 2013 and 2015, DeepMind worked on&nbsp;
an incredible learning algorithm by the name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:18.400
Deep Reinforcement Learning. This technique looked&nbsp;
at the pixels of the game, was given a controller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:24.800
and played much like a human would… with the&nbsp;
exception that it learned to play some Atari games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:30.960
on a superhuman level. I have tried to train it&nbsp;
a few years ago and would like to invite you for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:37.680
a marvelous journey to see what happened. When&nbsp;
it starts learning to play an old game, Atari&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:44.160
breakout, at first, the algorithm loses all of&nbsp;
its lives without any signs of intelligent action.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.560
If we wait a bit, it becomes better at playing&nbsp;
the game, roughly matching the skill level of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:58.960
adept player. But here's the catch, if we wait for&nbsp;
longer, we get something absolutely spectacular.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.960
Over time, it learns to play like a pro, and&nbsp;
finds out that the best way to win the game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.200
is digging a tunnel through the&nbsp;
bricks and hit them from behind.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.480
This technique is combination of a neural network&nbsp;
that processes the visual data that we see on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:20.240
the screen, and a reinforcement learner that&nbsp;
comes up with the gameplay-related decisions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:26.080
This is an amazing algorithm, a&nbsp;
true breakthrough in AI research.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:30.480
A key point in this work was that the&nbsp;
problem formulation here enabled us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:35.360
to measure our progress easily: we&nbsp;
hit one brick, we get some points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:43.920
so do a lot of that. Lose a few lives, the game&nbsp;
ends, don’t do that! Easy enough. But there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:51.040
other, exploration-based games like Montezuma’s&nbsp;
revenge or Pitfall that it was not good at. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:57.680
man, these games are a nightmare for any AI,&nbsp;
because there is no score, or at the very least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.200
it’s hard to define how well we are doing. Because&nbsp;
there are no scores, it is hard to motivate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:09.600
AI to do anything at all other than just wander&nbsp;
around aimlessly. If no one tells us if we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:17.600
doing well or not, which way do we go? Explore&nbsp;
this place or go to the next one? How do we solve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:23.600
all this? And with that, let’s discuss&nbsp;
the state of play in AIs playing difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:29.520
exploration-based computer games. And I think&nbsp;
you will love to see how far we have some since.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:35.760
First, there is a previous line of work that&nbsp;
infused these agents with a very human-like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:43.600
property… curiosity. That agent was able to do&nbsp;
much, much better at these games…and then got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:50.160
addicted to the TV. But that’s a different story.&nbsp;
Note that this TV problem has been remedied since.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:55.360
And this new method attempts&nbsp;
to solve hard exploration games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.880
by watching Youtube videos&nbsp;
of humans playing the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:06.080
and learning from that, as you see, it just rips&nbsp;
through these levels in Montezuma’s revenge and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:13.200
other games too. So, I wonder how does all this&nbsp;
magic happen? How did this agent learn to explore?

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.840
Well, it has three things going&nbsp;
for it that really makes this work.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:24.320
One, the Skeptical Scholar would say, that all is&nbsp;
takes is just copy-pasting what it saw from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:30.960
human player! Also, imitation learning is not new,&nbsp;
which is a point that we will address in a moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:37.200
so, why bother with this? Now, hold on&nbsp;
to your papers, and observe as it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:43.520
noticeably less efficient than the human&nbsp;
teacher was. Until we realize that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:49.040
is not the human player, and this is&nbsp;
not the AI…but the other way around!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:56.320
Look, it was so observant and took away so much&nbsp;
from the human demonstrations that in the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:03.680
it became even more efficient than its&nbsp;
human teacher. Whoa! Absolutely amazing.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.840
And while we are here, I would like&nbsp;
to dissect this copy-paste argument.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:13.680
You see, it has an understanding of the game,&nbsp;
and does not just copy the human demonstrator.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:21.120
But even if it just copied what it saw, it would&nbsp;
not be so easy because the AI only sees images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:28.080
and it has to translate how the images change in&nbsp;
response to us pressing buttons on the controller.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.480
We might also encounter the same&nbsp;
level, but at a different time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:37.120
and we have to understand how to vanquish&nbsp;
an opponent and how to perform that.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.440
Two, nobody hooked the agent&nbsp;
into the game information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:46.800
which is huge. This means that it doesn’t know&nbsp;
what buttons are pressed on the controller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:53.520
no internal numbers or the game state are given&nbsp;
to it, and most importantly, it is also not given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:05:00.320
the score of the game. We discussed how difficult&nbsp;
this makes everything. Unfortunately, this means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:06.320
that there is no easy way out - it really has to&nbsp;
understand what it sees and mine out the relevant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:13.360
information from each of these videos. And as you&nbsp;
see, it does that with flying colors. Loving it.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:20.160
And three, it can handle the domain gap. Previous&nbsp;
imitation learning methods did not deal with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:27.120
too well. So what does that mean? Let’s look at&nbsp;
this latent space together and find out. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:32.080
is what a latent space looks like if we just&nbsp;
embed the pixels that we see in the videos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:38.080
Don’t worry, I’ll tell you in a moment what that&nbsp;
is. Here, the clusters are nicely clumped up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:44.960
away from each other, so that’s probably good,&nbsp;
right? Well, in this problem, not so much!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:49.520
A latent space means a place where&nbsp;
similar kinds of data are meant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:55.280
be close to each other. These are snippets of the&nbsp;
demonstration videos that the clusters relate to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:01.680
Let’s test that together. Do you&nbsp;
think these images are similar? Yes?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:09.200
Most of us humans would say that these are quite&nbsp;
similar, in fact, they are nearly the same. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:16.800
is this a good latent space embedding? No,&nbsp;
not in the slightest. This data is similar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:22.480
therefore, these should be close to each other,&nbsp;
but this previous technique did not recognize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:29.520
because these images have slightly different&nbsp;
colors, aspect ratios, this has a text overlay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:35.280
but we all understand that despite all that, we&nbsp;
are looking at the same game through different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:43.360
windows. So, does the new technique recognize&nbsp;
that? Oh yes, beautiful. Praise the papers!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:49.840
Similar game states are now close to each&nbsp;
other, we can align them properly and therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:55.440
we can learn more easily from them. This is&nbsp;
one of the reasons why it can play so well.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:02.160
So there you go, these new AI agents can look&nbsp;
at how we perform complex exploration games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:09.600
and learn so well from us, that in the end, they&nbsp;
do even better than we do. And now, to get them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:16.400
write some amazing papers for us…or, you know, Two&nbsp;
Minute Papers episodes. What a time to be alive!

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:16.800
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

